WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.031 --> 00:00:07.674
Well the main difference between free software and other software is that

00:00:08.388 --> 00:00:12.867
other software is designed to put the developer in power

00:00:12.913 --> 00:00:15.134
and you as the user are kinda subjected to the power of the developer.

00:00:15.909 --> 00:00:18.565
What free software does, it kinda flips that on its head and says,

00:00:18.665 --> 00:00:21.929
well you as a user deserve the freedom

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:24.027
to use your computer to do the things that you want to do.

00:00:28.863 --> 00:00:29.861
I'm Matt Lee,

00:00:29.861 --> 00:00:31.856
I'm the -now- technology lead at creative commons.

00:00:32.447 --> 00:00:34.572
My background is really in sort of free software.

00:00:34.672 --> 00:00:35.627
Uhh..

00:00:35.627 --> 00:00:37.752
I used to work in the free software foundation for a long time.

00:00:38.398 --> 00:00:41.755
And I'm originally from the UK, but I now live in Boston in the United States.

00:00:42.046 --> 00:00:45.071
When we talk about free software we're talking about software that's free like free speech.

00:00:45.461 --> 00:00:48.891
And less so in the terms of free like a free beer or a free lunch with someone.

00:00:49.679 --> 00:00:53.242
And we've really kinda defined free software as having four characteristics.

00:00:53.738 --> 00:00:57.916
The first characteristic, freedom zero, is the freedom to run the program, for any purpose.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:01.838
Freedom one, is the freedom to modify the program.

00:01:02.456 --> 00:01:03.968
Freedom two, is the freedom to

00:01:03.993 --> 00:01:04.509
Uhh..

00:01:05.472 --> 00:01:06.472
Share the program.

00:01:06.497 --> 00:01:09.795
And freedom three is the freedom to share modified versions of the program.

00:01:10.359 --> 00:01:12.020
And if you have those four freedoms,

00:01:12.461 --> 00:01:13.680
you have free software.

00:01:13.711 --> 00:01:15.039
If you have one of those freedoms missing,

00:01:15.139 --> 00:01:16.535
Or two of those freedoms missing,

00:01:16.805 --> 00:01:19.155
you are not using free software.

00:01:19.541 --> 00:01:22.641
A good example of that would be something like iTunes.

00:01:23.369 --> 00:01:26.135
People think, "Well iTunes is free, I can download it from Apple's website."

00:01:26.235 --> 00:01:28.980
And it's like, well that's true, you can get it from Apple's website,

00:01:29.394 --> 00:01:30.865
but it's not free software. Because

00:01:31.135 --> 00:01:34.004
first of all, you can't run iTunes for any purpose.

00:01:34.445 --> 00:01:37.419
That document that you never read, when you click "I agree",

00:01:37.519 --> 00:01:39.132
says, quite explicitly,

00:01:39.137 --> 00:01:40.871
you can't use it for a lot of reasons.

00:01:40.871 --> 00:01:42.301
Apple takes things like,

00:01:42.387 --> 00:01:44.532
they'll make changes to iTunes

00:01:44.632 --> 00:01:49.007
when they find problems with their encryption scheme and DRM software.

00:01:49.107 --> 00:01:52.030
And they will just force those restrictions on users of iTunes,

00:01:52.076 --> 00:01:53.348
so the next time they open iTunes,

00:01:53.721 --> 00:01:55.846
it will refuse to do anything until you upgrade.

00:01:55.946 --> 00:01:56.843
And so,

00:01:56.943 --> 00:01:57.943
They are

00:01:58.043 --> 00:02:01.341
heavily restricting what you can and can't do with their software.

00:02:01.359 --> 00:02:05.400
If it was free software, anyone could improve iTunes and give it to anyone they wanted to.

00:02:05.739 --> 00:02:07.004
And they could use it to help other people

00:02:07.104 --> 00:02:09.809
but as it stands right now, only Apple can kinda

00:02:09.848 --> 00:02:11.011
make changes to iTunes.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:12.534
And therefore,

00:02:12.566 --> 00:02:14.861
we leave all of the users of iTunes without help.

00:02:15.261 --> 00:02:17.639
Without the ability to use software with freedom.

00:02:17.739 --> 00:02:21.507
Many people have used pieces of software over the years that no longer exist anymore

00:02:22.057 --> 00:02:24.871
and I think that if you invest time in using a piece of free software,

00:02:25.939 --> 00:02:29.528
you will ultimately be able to use that software for a very very long time to come.

00:02:29.539 --> 00:02:33.832
You can't be sure that Adobe will keep Photoshop going forever.

00:02:34.171 --> 00:02:36.242
It's probably not gonna happen anytime soon but,

00:02:36.274 --> 00:02:39.299
I could see a future where Adobe just stops selling Photoshop.

00:02:39.658 --> 00:02:41.264
Or the stop selling Illustrator.

00:02:41.323 --> 00:02:42.903
They've already done it with certain pieces of software.

00:02:42.914 --> 00:02:45.026
They bought Fireworks from Macromedia

00:02:45.412 --> 00:02:46.978
and they don't make Fireworks anymore

00:02:46.997 --> 00:02:49.251
so everyone who used Fireworks, for 20 years,

00:02:49.494 --> 00:02:51.013
no longer could use Fireworks,

00:02:51.801 --> 00:02:54.008
because Adobe decided to stop making that.

00:02:54.033 --> 00:02:55.224
Now if that was free software,

00:02:56.027 --> 00:02:59.867
Even though Adobe, who are maybe a corporate sponsor of this piece of software,

00:03:00.117 --> 00:03:01.314
were no longer supporting it,

00:03:02.075 --> 00:03:05.564
The community could still keep Fireworks going,

00:03:05.603 --> 00:03:08.089
and so it would be still being used.

00:03:08.871 --> 00:03:12.182
Under a free software model, you could go and get your copy of Photoshop

00:03:12.193 --> 00:03:14.059
from any number of vendors

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:18.142
who are all equally engaged in it's support and it's maintenance.

00:03:18.242 --> 00:03:21.335
And then you would choose the company that had the best offering

00:03:21.435 --> 00:03:26.100
So maybe here in Nottingham, there'd be a local company Gimp and Inkscape

00:03:26.888 --> 00:03:28.997
which are similar to Photoshop and Illustrator.

00:03:29.097 --> 00:03:31.767
And maybe they would offer a better offering,

00:03:31.799 --> 00:03:33.897
that would be more appropriate for a local person

00:03:33.902 --> 00:03:35.686
So there'd be workshop access and,

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:40.775
onsite training that maybe you would go with a local company vs the current choice

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.475
which is you get it from Adobe or you don't get it at all.

00:03:42.494 --> 00:03:43.807
That's the real kind of tragedy

00:03:45.087 --> 00:03:46.755
of proprietary software, it's that it

00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:50.222
ultimately leaves users dependant and then divided

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.229
and ultimately without a choice, and so,

00:03:52.329 --> 00:03:56.042
if you can spend anytime using free software,

00:03:56.108 --> 00:03:58.069
even if you use Photoshop

00:03:59.015 --> 00:04:00.233
maybe you could install Gimp.

00:04:00.333 --> 00:04:03.671
And the next time you have a thing you want to create

00:04:03.771 --> 00:04:04.771
try using Gimp.

00:04:04.871 --> 00:04:08.749
And if you get stuck, you could always save it in a Photoshop file

00:04:08.849 --> 00:04:10.851
and go away and kind of

00:04:11.762 --> 00:04:14.365
do what you need to do and maybe come back to Gimp.

00:04:14.779 --> 00:04:18.270
It's worth investing the time and effort into using something,

00:04:18.608 --> 00:04:22.785
that ultimately has your back vs the back of investors,

00:04:22.804 --> 00:04:25.248
and you know, Big Corporate

00:04:27.311 --> 00:04:29.750
[Camera dude] Okay so the elephant in the room,

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:32.848
are these reliable, or are these just for people who like getting under the hood of their computers?

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.978
I think that's the one thing that comes up.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:38.273
I would say that, generally speaking,

00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:41.099
the free software programs that you've heard of are reliable.

00:04:41.212 --> 00:04:45.219
There are of course, new versions of those software.

00:04:45.319 --> 00:04:50.394
And you can't always rely that the very latest version of something is gonna be reliable.

00:04:50.494 --> 00:04:52.265
But that's true too of any software

00:04:52.665 --> 00:04:54.040
I would say that,

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.463
if you go and you download a piece of software from,

00:04:56.481 --> 00:04:58.777
if you go and download Ubuntu for example,

00:04:59.034 --> 00:05:03.129
or Fedora or Debian or one of these distributions of free software

00:05:03.522 --> 00:05:06.540
and you install the current, latest release

00:05:06.885 --> 00:05:08.403
you're gonna have a pretty good time.

00:05:08.503 --> 00:05:10.356
Things are generally speaking gonna work well for you

00:05:10.456 --> 00:05:12.506
and you're gonna have the occasional thing where

00:05:13.147 --> 00:05:14.147
something doesn't quite work,

00:05:14.247 --> 00:05:15.765
you might have a webcam that doesn't quite work well

00:05:16.772 --> 00:05:19.286
or maybe your wireless card has

00:05:19.338 --> 00:05:22.343
needs extra, proprietary software to function

00:05:23.438 --> 00:05:26.300
and those are real problems, their problems that all computer users face.

00:05:27.614 --> 00:05:29.221
But when you use Windows or when you use a Mac

00:05:29.239 --> 00:05:30.321
those problems are kinda hidden away from you

00:05:30.353 --> 00:05:31.796
you don't really see them because,

00:05:32.542 --> 00:05:35.935
They don't mind adding extra proprietary programs

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:38.316
of off what you already have to make those things function.

00:05:38.982 --> 00:05:39.982
What I would say is this.

00:05:40.859 --> 00:05:41.573
If you have,

00:05:42.744 --> 00:05:44.637
a computer that you would like to try free software

00:05:44.737 --> 00:05:45.737
a lot of these distributions

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:49.005
make what's callled a liveCD, or a liveUSB

00:05:49.386 --> 00:05:50.386
you can download it

00:05:50.793 --> 00:05:53.818
onto a blank CD or a blank USB stick

00:05:54.245 --> 00:05:56.557
and just try it without actually installing it on your computer

00:05:56.582 --> 00:05:57.654
It'll be a little bit slower of course,

00:05:57.654 --> 00:05:59.541
cause it's running off the CD or USB stick.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:02.625
But it'll give you some sense of what works or what doesn't work.

00:06:03.202 --> 00:06:05.171
But yeah it'll give you a sense of the reliability too.

00:06:05.203 --> 00:06:09.044
And if you have an older computer that you don't use anymore,

00:06:09.044 --> 00:06:12.479
then by all means you can go ahead and install free software on there.

00:06:12.586 --> 00:06:18.025
It will work surprisingly well compared to the way that your Windows or your Mac used to function,

00:06:18.125 --> 00:06:19.398
When it was kinda on it's last legs.

00:06:20.037 --> 00:06:22.257
And I think you'll have a good time with it.

00:06:22.563 --> 00:06:30.786
(I'd like to point out that this video was subtitled with Free Software, specifically Subtitle Composer)

00:06:33.106 --> 00:06:37.176
Video editing isn't always the easiest thing to do with free software

00:06:37.208 --> 00:06:39.994
but it can be done, right? People do edited video with free software all the time.

00:06:40.414 --> 00:06:45.028
And you know, video editing software is somewhat in it's infancy, as free software.

